{
  "challenge": "SQL-001",
  "task": "The monthly report needs revenue by customer. Write the query.\n\nThe database (PostgreSQL) has one table, `orders`, with one row per order:\n\n| column | type | meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `id` | `integer`, primary key | the order's id |\n| `customer` | `text`, not null | the customer's name |\n| `total` | `integer`, not null | the order's total; can be 0, or negative for an adjustment |\n\nReturn one row per customer who has orders, with two columns in this order: the customer's name, and the sum of the totals of their orders. Order the rows by that sum, highest first, then by customer name ascending when sums tie (PostgreSQL’s default text ordering). Column names don't matter. Names match exactly as written, and a sum can be larger than a 32-bit integer holds. No orders gives no rows.\n",
  "language": "sql",
  "file": "queries/top-customers.sql",
  "starter": "-- Monthly report: revenue by customer, biggest first.\n-- orders(id, customer, total)\nSELECT customer, total\nFROM orders;\n",
  "entrypoint": "query",
  "schema": "CREATE TABLE orders (\n  id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer text NOT NULL,\n  total integer NOT NULL\n);\n",
